  "note": "cross_backend_equivalence pinned reference: folded resonator-subblock complex eigenvalues (np.sort_complex order) of the **python** backend (fuqtgui domain.*) for each synthesizable registered twoport case. The folded resonator-subblock eigenvalue multiset is a similarity invariant of the *internal* network (its natural frequencies), so it confirms two backends synthesized the same internal dynamics up to the contract's 1e-6 tolerance regardless of folding convention / plane rotation. NOTE: it is NOT invariant under a global internal sign flip M->-M (eig(-M)=-eig(M)); the book-anchored entry twoport_4_4 only matches its published matrix in the flip branch (see its oracle_note) - the gauge question is open, see ROADMAP. CAVEAT (1.1.0): this is a NECESSARY-NOT-SUFFICIENT fingerprint - it does not constrain external port couplings (R1/R2), so two genuinely different filters can share it; use alongside response_invariance, not as standalone proof of 'same filter'. ORACLE TIERS (P06): each entry declares 'oracle': 'published_matrix' (L1 - an independent book/CST printed folded matrix anchors the entry, enforced in CI by tests/test_cross_backend_reference.py::test_reference_is_book_anchored, gauge-aware tolerance 2e-4) or 'reference_backend' (L2 - no published matrix; the entry is the reference backend's own output and is only cross-checked by the other backend). Provenance: generated by fuqtgui src/scripts/bench_synthesis.py PythonBackend on 2026-10-07 (CST entries 2026-10-20 via the same synthesize->coupling_matrix_to_folded_cm->eigvals(folded[1:-1,1:-1]) path); regenerate when the python domain synthesis/folding code changes, then re-run the book-anchor test.",
